From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] steamcompmgr: recover the virtual white y on ARM64

Steam's color temperature slider sets GAMESCOPE_DISPLAY_VIRTUAL_WHITE
to a white point (x, y). The ARM64 client passes the two floats as one
64-bit property element, so gamescope reads x and a zero y, and a white
point with y = 0 is ignored: the slider did nothing on ARM64. The same
packing fault is behind the night mode workaround.

Steam derives the pair from the CIE daylight locus (its 6500 K x is
D65's), so when y is missing recover it from x with the locus quadratic,
within 0.002 of the true value across the slider's range; outside that
range the point is left invalid as before.

---
diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
index e54194b..4131467 100644
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6940,6 +6940,14 @@ handle_property_notify(xwayland_ctx_t *ctx, XPropertyEvent *ev)
 		{
 			g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.x = santitize_float( bit_cast<float>( user_vec[0] ) );
 			g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.y = santitize_float( bit_cast<float>( user_vec[1] ) );
+#if defined(__aarch64__)
+			// The ARM64 Steam client packs the pair as one 64-bit element, so only x arrives;
+			// Steam derives the pair from the CIE daylight locus, so y follows from x.
+			// Drop once the client sends both floats (y arrives non-zero).
+			float x = g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.x;
+			if ( x >= 0.25f && x <= 0.55f && g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.y <= 0.01f )
+				g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.y = -3.0f * x * x + 2.87f * x - 0.275f;
+#endif
 		}
 		else
 		{
